<div class="paragraph" style="--accent: var(--color-hl1);">
    <h2>meteor shower</h2>
    <p>2026-09</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
    <p>the alcove door cracked and slowly slid open, the inside lit up. a highly sophisticated piece of technology inside powered up, took a step forward... and lazily slumped to the floor. with some struggle she lifted herself up by her arms to sit up, then rubbed her eyes and let them adjust to the dark room. it was kind of weird to suddenly start existing, to have thoughts for the first time. not to mention a precise clock that kept ticking in her head now. 3251 september 27th. the date surprised her.</p>
    <p>while trying to stand up, holding onto the flimsy alcove door, she looked around the room. the floor was covered in random mechanical components and tools, they looked like they had been thrown around, what wasn't tied down still got a lot of hits, including the other alcoves in the room. there were 3 more, all with robots of their own, though inactive. only thing that was broken on her was her memory luckily. she took a few uneasy steps forward, trying to not slip on a screwdriver, and went exploring.</p>

    <p>most other rooms looked the same, like there was some kind of impact and everything got jostled. Metal frames, spools of wire, fertilizer, seeds. this is a colony-builder ship, one that arrives before the actual colonists do. it would make sense from the supplies. that must make her a construction drone. it also means she'll have to do everything on her own, judging by the state of her colleagues. fuuuck. according to the computer log, the ship collided with a debris field during the journey. it would explain all the broken equipment, luckily the ship managed to land in one piece, but she was worried that there wouldn't be enough time to build a working habitat before arrival.</p>
    <p>she recorded a message and set it to broadcast to the second ship on loop. "technical issues, habitat not built yet, don't wake up colonists". there was no time to waste, she found an airlock and went exploring for suitable locations.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
    <p>it was a nice little planet. gravity a bit stronger than earth, blue-ish rocky landscape, nitrogen-rich atmosphere. there was a wide flat plane right next to the ship luckily, only filled with a few rocks that would need to be cleared away to make space for the foundations. the first few days she really rushed everything, managed to trip and hurt herself a few times, eventually she calmed down and worked fast but carefully. rushing will only make her forget to check something and have a fatal accident later on.</p>

    <p>it took her over a year to drill into the ground and set in a solid foundation, some more months to drag out all the metal frames outside the ship and screw them together. then came the wiring. she blew a fuse on one of the battery packs and broke a solar panel but there were enough spares, by this point she also learned enough to repair herself properly, even made some tune ups on her muscles. year 2 came and still no sign of the second ship. hopefully they heard the message and slowed down their approach.</p>
    <p>eventually her daily routine became more relaxed, instead of working the full day and recharging through the night, she took breaks, explored the landscape. at night she liked to watch the sky, trying to identify the different planets in the system. every 4 or so months there came a great meteor shower which lit up the sky, it really surprised her the first time, she hadn't seen something this beautiful. it must've been from a recent impact of some metallic object, judging by the colors and the trajectory of the fragments.</p>

    <p>she was mostly done with the habitat around year 4. if the colonists arrived now they would be able to move in and populate it, but there was radio silence. she changed the radio message, even tried running the radar to look for something in orbit, maybe the ship went into power saving mode and just waited for a ping, but nothing. instead of twiddling her thumbs, she decided to try repairing one of the other drones, it was getting lonely.</p>
        
    <p>she had to mix components from the different units, seemed appropriate to combine their two numbers and give them to her new friend. when twelve woke up, she immediately started panicking; she woke up late according to the clock, she should be building the habitat ! worse, the colonists should've arrived a year ago already. are they safe? is everything prepared? why is she even activated this late? was there an accident- four jumped in with a hug to calm twelve down. squeezed her tight, told her everything will be okay. she explained the situation, that there's still no colonists, but the habitat is built, but it needs some furniture still. she asked if she wanted to help with the kitchen cabinets.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
    <p>year 8. the first few months were a little rough, twelve was still worried, just like four was, but over time she calmed down, kept busy by checking and re-doing some wiring, then started helping four with some bed frames. the habitat would've been cramped for the incoming 30 person crew, but for the two of them it was more than enough space to work and play in. they later picked up botany as a hobby, cultivating new plants from the seeds they had onboard. four was especially proud of the strawberry-lemon hybrid, the fruits were a vibrant orange and filled with juice. she had no way of knowing if tasted any good but that didn't matter to them much. she liked to pick a flower now and then and stick it into twelve's synthetic hair, then take both of them to the habitat roof and stare at the sky, especially when the meteor shower came. they barely thought about the colonists anymore, they probably never left earth in the first place.</p>

    <p>high above TOI-1338d, the scattered remains of a colony ship and its occupants kept orbiting on a highly elliptical path, a period of 126 days. on every fly-by, a portion of the cloud dipped into the atmosphere, and fell down to the surface aflame, lighting up the sky.</p>
</div>